nt is  mandated  proficiency testing is mandated most  countries mandate  some kind of licensing or accreditation  external accreditation where uh you're  given a set of requirements and you have  to meet them  but there are some internal appraisal  processes your laboratory should have  and  quality assurance meaning quality  indicators is one of them  do you measure processes not because you  have to but because you want to know  what's going on  and the second one is it is part of the  international medical laboratory  standard and not  um as popular in the united states as it  could be or should be it's called  internal audit but the laboratory  community is moving in that direction  and that's where  your laboratory's internal auditors  look at your own processes watch people  do work  and see if they're going according to  the approved  process and procedure documents and are  you getting the outcomes  from those processes that you have  planned for and you expect  so prevention is designed an appraisal  some of that's given to you by  requirement  and some of it you do because you want  to know how well you're doing  and that's when you'll know you have  enough  i hope that answers your question  thank you so much and lucy is there a  way to quantify  external failure costs  as i mentioned the external failure  costs are  big lawsuits  um misdiagnoses  those are huge let's start with the  smaller one like a complaint  a complaint is a non-conforming event  your laboratory is required  to have a means to capture  complaints if you capture complaints i  mentioned earlier in the program  part of that reporting form could just  be a little tick box  about how much time you the  people different people involved in the  complaint spent  resolving their part of the complaint  and that can give you  a way to calculate the cost of a of a  complaint  you can also calculate the cost of a  corrected report  you can use the failure cost worksheet  for the labor part where you can cap you  can calculate the cost  to rectify rectify a corrected report  based on the different job titles  involved  those are two of the external failure  costs that are easier  when it comes to  misdiagnoses and and lawsuits  i i don't think you can even begin to  calculate  the volume of money involved in that  because  as soon as the organization's legal  aspect gets involved the costs are  astronomical and that's about the best  answer i can give you go for what you  know  you can do complaints and corrected  reports  and those are the most common  laboratory external failures  thank you so much lucy and i want to  thank our audience members for these  great questions  coming in we have time for a few more  where do i  record root causes analysis  and corrective actions costs in my  worksheet  well it's a good question but the  worksheet's not meant to calculate  root cause analysis and corrective  action  a root cause analysis is a project  there's going to be a set of people  you're going to look at the existing  process that did not work correctly  you're going to dissect that process to  find out  what went wrong and why it went wrong  this is usually a series of meetings  sometimes these meetings don't take very  long and you can nail the root cause  fairly quickly  and sometimes there are people involved  in the root cause  outside the laboratory's walls like  nurses perhaps and surgeons or radiology  or messengers  hospital messengers or nurse  patient care technician personnel and  they're hard to get involved  in a root cause analysis project that  emanates from the laboratory  so you can't use the worksheet for these  and it will be difficult to calculate  the cost of a root cause analysis  project but i'll tell you it's going to  be high  why because we're talking about labor  we're not talking about yellow stickies  on the or  on brown paper or the cost of the  markers to work on  whiteboard we're talking about people  sitting in meetings for  hours until we determine the root cause  so  good question but that's not what the  worksheet's meant for  thank you so much our next audience  member asks  i know i will always have some qc  failures in the future  which i have a troubleshoot how can i  include this in a laboratory  budget  okay no laboratory is perfect  and you're right there will be some qc  failures  as time goes on so how do you know how  um how do you put them in the budget  i would calculate the cost of one  failure  you look at your historic experience so  if you've been keeping track of these  over the months and years you should  have a pretty good idea  how often this happens if you're not  keeping track  you can't guess so you're going to have  to calculate  proactively by starting today  and keeping track as time goes on how  many did we have this month  and next month if you have historical  data you can look back  um so you could calculate on the  worksheet what one of them costs  look at your historic experience but you  know what  you don't want to have those failures at  all so  it's not like you want to plan to put  a lot of money in qc failure into your  budget  so you look at how many have happened if  you have historical data and say  is this a reasonable number is this an  unreasonable number  can this number be reduced and  unfortunately in the global laboratory  community  there are no benchmarks there's no  published  data on what people's experience with  failed  failed qc runs or failed calibration  of incomplete test runs invalid test run  nobody reports those in a journal or  puts  that information online nobody's going  to air their dirty laundry in public  so there's no way for you to gauge  except by your laboratory's historical  experience  so take your historical experience  calculate one failure cost  but ask yourself the question is this  reasonable experience  can we reduce it that's our threshold  make that  number go down and that's about all i  can share with you  um on that because there's no published  data  thank you so much we have time for one  more question lucy  can we just assume that failure costs  are gonna be a part of  the process  okay well i'm sorry  but that sounds to me like you're  planning for failure  instead of planning for quality  so i don't want you to assume failure  costs are part of your process  prevention is design laboratory  management is responsible for designing  the pre-examination examination and  post-examination processes  to meet requirements regulatory  requirements accreditation requirements  cust reasonable customer requirements  and any organizational requirements  that's labs management's responsibility  to design those processes  if you're not designing your processes  you're not doing your job correctly and  so if you're focusing  on assuming failure costs are part of  your process you've got the whole thing  backwards so let me share a little quick  little story with you  this is true a united states company  contracted  with a company in japan to make some  parts  for them they wanted ten thousand parts  with a zero point  zero  percent error that's three parts and 10  000 parts they didn't want any more  error than that  us says to japan make us these parts  with this specification  so when the order arrives at the u.s  company they open up the box  and on top of the box of 10 000 parts is  a little bitty box  and inside that box are three pieces  that are out of specification and the  japanese said  well we gave you what you wanted and we  don't understand why you wanted us to  make three pieces that didn't fit  but this is what your specification was  so this is what we're giving you  do you see what that means there that  was planning for  failure but the japanese planned for no  failure  and they achieved no failure and then  curious as to why the us would want  parts out of spec  gave them what they wanted even though  that's not what they needed  so again i'll encourage planning  prevention is planning and design that's  where  laboratory management needs to focus on  every single process  in the laboratory and i hope that  answers your question you might not like  it  that's a great answer lucy thank you  again for this  incredible presentation and for your  time today  and before we go i want to thank our  audience members for joining us today  and for their questions  questions we did not have time for today  will be answered and posted in the event  resources by november 27th  and this webcast will be available on  demand in the auditorium from friday on  if you found this informative and  helpful please share it with your  colleagues  and please check back regularly on the  topics  and remember to take a look at the  booths in the exhibit hall  of the virtual conference until next  time take care  be safe stay healthy bye
 

